# 1. Analysis & Pre-Thinking

## Core Requirements

**Request 1 — Project-scoped base path**
Every route must carry the *escaped* active project name as its first URL segment:
- `/chats` → `/my.project/chats`
- `/desktop` → `/my-project/desktop`
- `/settings` → `/codx-junior/settings`

"Escaped" = `encodeURIComponent()` when *building* a URL (raw, so dots/dashes/spaces stay valid) and `decodeURIComponent()` when *reading* it (Vue Router already decodes matched params, so only the write-side needs encoding).

**Request 2 — Project selector modal dies in DesktopSidebar**
In `AppsSection.vue` the selector is rendered with `v-if="!isCollapsed"`. The desktop sidebar toggles `sidebarCollapsed` on **collapse/expand and on hover float**. That flips `isCollapsed`, so `<ProjectDetailt>` is **unmounted and re-created**, throwing away its internal `isModalOpen` state. Worse, the floating sidebar listens to `@mouseleave`; moving the cursor toward the modal leaves the wrapper → sidebar collapses → the component that owns the modal is destroyed → modal vanishes.

## Strategic Approach

| # | Problem | Strategy |
|---|---------|----------|
| 1 | Project prefix on routes | (a) Prefix every `path` in `router/index.ts` with `/:projectName` + add a `root` bootstrap route. (b) Enforce the prefix **once**, centrally, by wrapping `router.push`/`router.replace` so any named navigation auto-injects the escaped project segment (covers `navigate.js`, `config/apps.js`, `RouterLink`, and any stray call site). (c) Keep the URL in sync when the active project changes. (d) Fix the two places that hard-code the un-scoped root. |
| 2 | Selector modal remount | Swap `v-if` → `v-show` on the selector wrapper so the component stays mounted and its state (and modal) survives collapse/expand and hover float. The `Modal` teleports its DOM into the app root (`Modal.vue` → `this.$root.$el.appendChild`), so hiding the wrapper does **not** hide an open modal. |

## Dependencies & Risks
- **Interceptors must not touch string paths / the `root` route** — `withProject()` only injects for *named* routes whose record contains `:projectName`.
- **Redirects returned from guards bypass `router.push`** → those returns are updated individually in `navigate.js`.
- **URL ↔ project sync loop risk**: `syncRouteProjectPrefix()` is a **no-op** when the URL already matches the active project, and it is *not* called from inside the guard (which would conflict with an in-flight navigation). Deep links are resolved at boot in `restoreLastActiveProject()` instead.
- **`root` bootstrap**: while no project is active, `/` renders the home view; once a project loads the URL is promoted to `/:projectName`.

## Target Files

| File | Action |
|------|--------|
| `src/router/index.ts` | Modify |
| `src/router/navigate.js` | Modify |
| `src/views/QuickChatView.vue` | Modify (targeted) |
| `src/store/project.js` | Modify (targeted) |
| `src/components/layout/AppsSection.vue` | Modify |

---

# 2. Minimum Changes Approach

- **`src/router/index.ts`** — prefix all paths with `/:projectName`, add `root`, add `withProject()` interceptor + `scopedRouteNames` detection.
- **`src/router/navigate.js`** — add `activeProjectSegment()` / `homeLocation()` helpers; return scoped locations from the guard; add `getProjectName()`.
- **`src/views/QuickChatView.vue`** — `isHomePage` must treat `home` and `root`.
- **`src/store/project.js`** — add `syncRouteProjectPrefix()` action + call it on project change; resolve deep-link project on boot.
- **`src/components/layout/AppsSection.vue`** — `v-if` → `v-show` (the Request-2 fix).

Please validate this approach before I proceed to any follow-up (e.g. scoping desktop panels by project).

---

# 3. File changes

### `src/router/index.ts` (full file)

```ts src/router/index.ts
